 ],
 "literal": "In the many night watches when dew matured / disheveled head hair getting wet / in the boundary of the beautiful palace where he sweetly sleeps / to remove my suffering, approaching playing my kinai drum / while those who eat sacrificial offerings protect / saying 'may the virtuous-hearted one live long' / praising his coming for that / he of the hands that are hollowed and generous without withholding / those who obtained the name 'may you live' / saying 'there is no comparison to others' / thinking that, my mind becoming confused / seeing me who stood there / the traveling kinai drummer of a distant country / saying 'you are the protector to us' / mountain-yielded gems and forest-yielded gold / and sea-yielded radiant pearls / and diverse garments and thick-dreg jars of toddy / like seeing in a dream, to remain without suffering / he who granted in reality, the lord abundant in splendor / those who say 'country' say his country / those who say 'king' say his king / ... those with golden-tusked elephants / swift galloping saddled good horses / well-crafted bells on curved wheels / ... those of the good chariot hosts / swift sounding brave ones / those who live by the sword gathering with eagerness / the army having the sound of the sea / may you endure for a very long time, fierce lord!",
 "translation": "In the deep night watches when the dew thickens, wetting my disheveled hair, I approached the gates of your beautiful palace where you sleep peacefully. To banish my grief, I played my kinai drum. While the gods who receive sacrificial offerings protect you, I praised your long life, saying, 'May the virtuous-hearted king live for many days!' You, whose hollowed hands are ever generous without withholding, have earned the fame of being wished long life by all. Thinking that there is no equal to you, my mind was dazed. Seeing me stand there, a traveling drummer from a distant land, you said, 'I am your protector.' Then, you granted me gems from the mountains, gold from the forests, radiant pearls from the sea, fine garments, and jars of thick toddy, making my dream-like wishes a reality so that I may live without suffering. O splendid lord! When people speak of a true country, they speak of your country. When they speak of a true king, they speak of you. Surrounded by hosts of golden-tusked elephants, swift galloping horses, chariots with well-crafted bells on curved wheels, brave warriors who live by the sword gathering eagerly, and an army as loud as the roaring sea—may you, our fierce and victorious lord, endure for a very long time!",
 "modern": "பனி முதிர்ந்த நள்ளிரவில், கலைந்த என் தலைமயிர் நனையுமாறு, நீ இனிதே உறங்கும் அழகிய அரண்மனை வாயிலை அடைந்தேன். என் வறுமையைப் போக்க என் கிணைப் பறையை முழக்கினேன். தேவர்கள் உன்னைக் காக்க, 'அறநெஞ்சம் கொண்ட அரசன் பல்லாண்டு வாழ்க!' என்று வாழ்த்திப் பாடினேன். வஞ்சனையின்றி வாரி வழங்கும் வள்ளல் நீ. உனக்கு இணையானவர் எவருமில்லை என்று வியந்து நின்ற என்னை, தூரத்து நாட்டிலிருந்து வந்த பாணன் என்று அறிந்து, 'நானே உனக்குக் காவலன்' என்று கூறினாய். மலையில் பிறந்த மணிகளையும், காட்டில் விளைந்த பொன்னையும், கடலில் கிடைத்த ஒளிவீசும் முத்துகளையும், சிறந்த ஆடைகளையும், கள் நிறைந்த சாடிகளையும் கனவில் கண்டது போலன்றி, நனவிலேயே எனக்கு வழங்கி என் வறுமையைப் போக்கினாய். சிறந்த நாட்டைப் பற்றிப் பேசுவோர் உன் நாட்டையே கூறுவர்; சிறந்த அரசனைப் பற்றிப் பேசுவோர் உன்னையே கூறுவர். பொன்னிறக் கொம்புகளை உடைய யானைகளும், குதிரைகளும், மணிகள் ஒலிக்கும் தேர்களும், வாளேந்திப் போரிடும் வீரர்களும் கொண்ட கடல் போன்ற படையையுடைய கொற்றவனே! நீ பல்லாண்டு காலம் நிலைபெற்று வாழ்வாயாக!",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai (praising a patron's greatness) and is a blessing (Vāḻttiyal) addressed to the Chola king Perunaṟkiḷḷi by the poet Ulōccaṉār. The poet describes himself as a poor kinai drummer who arrives at the palace gates in the freezing, dewy midnight, his hair soaked with dew. He plays his drum and blesses the king, wishing him a long life protected by the gods. Dazed by the king's peerless reputation, the poet is warmly welcomed by the king, who declares himself the drummer's protector. The king then showers him with precious gifts—gems, gold, pearls, fine clothes, and toddy—turning his dream-like hopes into reality. The poet concludes by praising the king's country, his unmatched sovereignty, and his mighty army, wishing him an enduring and long reign.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}